# Machine-local state never enters the tree (founding spec riders; the
# committed device-id on PR #44 is the miss that earned this carrier).
.local/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# HARNESS MACHINERY, never commit material (kogaki#299). Agent worktrees are
# created and removed by the harness, and each one is a checkout of some other
# session's branch — a run that swept them into a commit would be committing
# another session's working tree. This is the same class as the block above
# rather than the repo-visible one below: nobody reads these where they work,
# they are simply state the tree should never have been offered.
.claude/worktrees/

# The Full Report's OWNER RENDERING (SPEC.md §2.5.2, SPEC-terrain §12.2 v11,
# kogaki#234). Repo-VISIBLE so the owner reads it where they work; NOT
# committed, because it derives from uncommitted survey records and inherits
# their sensitivity — visibility and publication are two decisions and this
# line is the second one, made explicitly rather than by where the file landed.
reports/

# The SHAPE READ (specs/spec-client-kit/SPEC.md §3.3, kogaki#325) — the second
# member of exactly that class, and installed by policy/kit/install.sh step 4d
# into every kit consumer. Repo-VISIBLE so every session and the owner read it
# where they work; NOT committed, because it derives from hub owner-realm
# material and this repository is public — committing it would be a
# declassification act the spec grants no grounds for.
policy/shape.md

# The MOVE-INGESTION SPECIMEN (specs/spec-draft-pipeline/SPEC.md §6.9.0,
# kogaki#223; dispositioned kogaki#299) — the THIRD member of that class, and
# the only one an owner writes by hand. §6.9.0 binds the input grammar to this
# file as its measured specimen and records it as "owner-authored, deliberately
# untracked"; this line is that clause's declaration in the carrier that
# enforces it. Repo-VISIBLE because the owner authors it where they work; NOT
# committed, because an admitted Move lands one file in `moves/` through the
# ingestion command's own review screen, and §6.9's "ADMISSION IS THE OWNER'S
# ACT AT THAT SCREEN, never the command's" is exactly what committing the input
# would route around — it would admit by location what the spec reserves for a
# gate. ANCHORED with a leading slash: the clause binds the REPOSITORY-ROOT
# file, and a bare `moves.md` would also exclude `moves/moves.md` — silently
# un-addable, which is the admission-by-omission this comment argues against.
/moves.md

# THE PRIVATE HARNESS SURFACE (kogaki#615, owner ruling 2026-08-22). `.claude`
# is machine-local by default: the repository is public, and harness state —
# hook registrations, pipeline and project config, skill files — is private
# unless a file genuinely must be shared. Default-deny: the directory's
# contents are excluded, and a path stays tracked ONLY through an explicit
# re-include below carrying its public-need argument. The registered boundary
# guard kogaki#618 (PENDING — not landed at this head; until it lands, a
# `git add -f` bypass fails nowhere) asserts every tracked `.claude/` path has
# its re-include here. History predating the ruling stays published —
# untracking removes the files from the tree going forward, not from history.
#
# THE PUBLIC-NEED CRITERION, applied rather than asserted: a file stays tracked
# when THIS REPOSITORY'S OWN VERIFICATION CANNOT RUN WITHOUT IT. A registered
# check that reads a path binds a contract to it, and CI runs on a fresh clone
# — so an untracked path under a tracked assertion is not a private file, it is
# a red suite on every clone. Each re-include below names the reader that makes
# it public. Prose MENTIONS do not qualify: `.claude/skills/review-lane/SKILL.md`
# is named in a handful of tracked files and READ by none — every occurrence is
# a prose mention or a path-glob literal, and no reader opens it — so it stayed
# private, and kogaki#630 retired the file itself.
#
# THE COUNT IS DELIBERATELY NOT A NUMBER ANY MORE (kogaki#632; PR #637 round 1).
# It read "9 tracked files", was 6 at that PR's base and 5 at its head, and it
# moved every time an unrelated sitting reworded a mention. The criterion the
# number was stated through — no reader opens the path — is what does the work
# and is unchanged; the figure was a fact that changes without a review point,
# which `specs/SPEC.md` already forbids a record from stating. Earlier forms
# said "three" and then "9"; both were true when written.
.claude/*

# SPEC.md §4's no-owner-override clause grounds the review rounds-bound on this
# site being TRACKED — "the same edit to a machine-local file would leave
# nothing to review" (specs/SPEC.md:1183-1188) — and the Review lane's
# mechanism declaration landed here as a tracked declaration (PR #617). A
# machine-local copy would make the bound unraisable-by-diff, which is the one
# property the clause exists to guarantee.
!.claude/review-lane.json

# The two skill files a registered check READS. Each carries a contract with no
# second carrier, and the check is the enforcement: `check-brief-entry.sh:796`
# asserts /brief's completion stages against its skill (the 2026-08-18 dogfood
# stopped at the mint and only the owner noticed), and
# `check-terrain-composition.sh:473` asserts the co-tag step is named in
# terrain's (its absence routed a tag selection to a second `view --tag` and
# produced the dump, §6.1).
!.claude/skills/
.claude/skills/*
!.claude/skills/brief/
!.claude/skills/terrain/
.claude/skills/brief/*
.claude/skills/terrain/*
!.claude/skills/brief/SKILL.md
!.claude/skills/terrain/SKILL.md

# The remaining reader, admitted by the same criterion (kogaki#621).
# Tracking it is what lets its absence guard be deleted rather than placed:
# `check-client-kit-install.sh` compares the kit source against the installed
# copy — the assertion does not exist without its file, and a guard standing
# in for it can hide more than it states (PR #620 round 2, where an `exit 0`
# guard silently skipped the check's primary job on every clone).
#
# THE HOOK RE-INCLUDE IS GONE (kogaki#630; PR #631 round 1, finding 4). This
# block used to admit `.claude/hooks/review-trigger.py` on the argument that
# `check-review-trigger.sh` ran its fixture pass over it. #630 deletes BOTH,
# so the argument named two absent files while the re-include kept admitting
# a path that no longer exists. `check-claude-boundary.sh` asserts only the
# subset direction (tracked ⊆ allowlist), so this passed mechanically and its
# own header routes exactly this case to a reader — which is where it was
# caught. The `.claude/hooks/` directory re-include goes with it: the
# directory now holds nothing tracked.
!.claude/skills/consult-first/
.claude/skills/consult-first/*
!.claude/skills/consult-first/SKILL.md
